#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535126} :activity/id #uuid "9cb47b16-2af6-58f1-8c90-123557ce104c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051034 :topic/id #uuid "7c092bea-3614-5bca-b402-7742647e083b" :topic/math-academy-id 6399} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2414084d-978d-5852-9a75-b14f36d5dc64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481869} :answer-field/id #uuid "817219d2-4fac-5374-9c4a-6822ef9e7011" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b679574-cdae-5555-900f-72dfd634fac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334797" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.25$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.25)≈0.125.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}=(2.5)^{3}=15.625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.125}{15.625}=0.008.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈0.008.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "003358a9-f2af-59ba-b62d-17d3096b53b8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308665 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05cb8cbf-2525-5aa2-adfe-cd481e3a035f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305203} {:answer/id #uuid "21bfa1f7-f19d-56ce-b7f8-e855ea310574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305475} {:answer/id #uuid "2eabd2c9-c08e-5c90-8a9a-e97ba53e2e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305595} {:answer/id #uuid "7458f81a-c783-575e-87db-c6a1d925d0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306256} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4785ce-99ec-52cb-ac88-d882b4bd6fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306484}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305475} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a56bc7-802d-5130-a340-47dd3d54165b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b8e9fee6-366d-51d7-82a2-1629aefe0b96" :question/math-academy-id "q-334647" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.15,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.15)=0.54.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.05)=0.003.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.15$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.15}{0.05}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.54$ to $0.003.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.54}{0.003}=180.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈180 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(180) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.726… \\\\ p & ≈3.726….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $4.$"} :step/id #uuid "27637d35-ea70-516e-ae4d-2f2b9a81ce00" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41acf3ec-3f1c-53ba-b201-c8a37ecb1876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534390} {:answer/id #uuid "44ca1322-93dc-59cc-b6ff-a1b25b2d993e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534395} {:answer/id #uuid "6f38908e-4bd6-5384-9925-30477dcb4253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534498} {:answer/id #uuid "a9dc44c0-ba2d-5163-911a-1574cea8f06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534644} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a3190f-7df3-50ea-a76a-7f1032b8c225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534676}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534390} :answer-field/id #uuid "986ff1d6-a817-5ff7-8aed-97a0eafbf95e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c354aefb-fffc-5ecc-8cac-e2625bd63b47" :question/math-academy-id "q-334744" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{4}+0.5(Δx)^{5}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{4}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{4}+0.5(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $11(Δx)^{4}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.2Δx.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "77786643-b29c-5c9c-ac94-7ecbfe592858" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535112}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535112 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d077e1b-69a5-5689-b7f2-27863f84eb6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305418}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305418} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9664d18-5c13-5c23-a303-4d40a0de2cc7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d047c5c7-9757-5ee5-9e01-a79118905089" :question/math-academy-id "q-334796" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.15$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.15)≈0.0225.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.15$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.15}{0.10}=1.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}=(1.5)^{2}=2.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.0225}{2.25}=0.01.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "77da484d-f99e-564b-b4c3-19d24cde8a96" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d20c43b-aebe-57e6-9524-dcd0d1e5d276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305899} {:answer/id #uuid "4e918a44-c564-5fed-a0ad-e307dda2bb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305917} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb35312-b7af-5bdf-9c23-68c4c07a3a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306191} {:answer/id #uuid "ba45bf27-6034-5a2b-b224-609af04300e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306888} {:answer/id #uuid "f1834450-0072-5bfe-83d8-37d9f92b8791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306888} :answer-field/id #uuid "346676e0-48f8-5066-ac65-2d01b43c9ccf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07099fdd-1348-5e3a-a68e-3d7b94b8ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333702" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.13.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.02,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02)=0.002.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.08$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.08}{0.02}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.13$ to $0.002.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.13}{0.002}=65.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈65 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(65) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.011… \\\\ p & ≈2.011….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "c4ecef28-9728-5f9f-986f-392086d6b9d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535102}}] :activity/title "Order of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534259}]}
